GeneralsX @feature iOS port 11/11/2026 SDL_GetWindowSizeInPixels follows the Metal view's scale.

On iOS SDL computes a window's pixel size as its view's points times the screen's native scale,
and gives its Metal view (SDL_Metal_CreateView, which DXVK's surface uses) that same scale once,
when it makes it. The external display support (ios/Launcher/GXLauncher, ExternalDisplay.swift)
moves the game's window to a monitor and lowers the Metal view's scale so the drawable is at most
about 1080p; the window's pixel size must say the same, or DXVK presents into a region of the
window's size that the smaller swapchain does not have, and the engine sizes its backbuffer wrong.
With no change to the layer's scale this reports exactly what SDL reported before.
Applied by cmake/sdl3.cmake (iOS only).

--- a/src/video/uikit/SDL_uikitwindow.m
+++ b/src/video/uikit/SDL_uikitwindow.m
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
 #include "SDL_uikitappdelegate.h"
 #include "SDL_uikitview.h"
 #include "SDL_uikitopenglview.h"
+#include "SDL_uikitmetalview.h"
 
 #include <Foundation/Foundation.h>
 
@@ -454,7 +455,16 @@
 #else
             scale = 2.0;
 #endif
+        }
+
+#if defined(SDL_VIDEO_VULKAN) || defined(SDL_VIDEO_METAL)
+        /* A Metal view's drawable is its bounds times its layer's scale (SDL_uikitmetalview.m), which
+         * the app may set apart from the screen's: report that, so the window's pixel size and the
+         * drawable agree. Unchanged otherwise: SDL gives the layer the scale computed above. */
+        if ([view isKindOfClass:[SDL_uikitmetalview class]]) {
+            scale = view.layer.contentsScale;
         }
+#endif
 
 
